                "event": "Flash Flood Warning",
                "sender": "w-nws.webmaster@noaa.gov",
                "senderName": "NWS Charleston SC",
                "headline": "Flash Flood Warning issued October 10 at 7:23AM EDT until October 10 at 9:30AM EDT by NWS Charleston SC",
                "description": "At 723 AM EDT, City of Charleston Emergency Management reported heavy\nrain in Downtown Charleston. Flash flooding is already occurring with\nseveral road closures across the city.\n\nHAZARD...Flash flooding caused by thunderstorms.\n\nSOURCE...City of Charleston Emergency Management reported.\n\nIMPACT...Flash flooding of small creeks and streams, urban areas,\nhighways, streets and underpasses as well as other poor\ndrainage and low-lying areas.\n\nSome locations that will experience flash flooding include...\nNorth Charleston, Mount Pleasant, James Island, Johns Island, West\nAshley, Downtown Charleston, Fort Sumter, Daniel Island, Folly\nBeach and Kiawah Island.\n\nAn area of intense rainfall moved through the Folly Beach, James\nIsland, Johns Island, West Ashley, and Downtown Charleston areas and\nproduced 1 to 2 inches of rain. The heaviest rain has now lifted\nnorthward but light to occasionally moderate rain will continue.\nAdditional rounds of heavy rain could develop through mid morning.\nCity of Charleston Emergency Management reported several roads closed\nacross the Downtown Charleston area and flash flooding is ongoing.\nAdditionally, a inundation depth sensor on Ogier Street near Calhoun\nStreet reported an inundation depth of over half of a foot.",
                "instruction": "Turn around, don't drown when encountering flooded roads. Most flood\ndeaths occur in vehicles.\n\nReport flooding directly to the Charleston National Weather Service\nat 1-888-383-2024 or email your reports to nws.charlestonsc@noaa.gov.",
                "response": "Avoid",
                "note": null,
                "parameters": {
